{
  "_comment": "OUR classification, not an official one. Two curated maps that serve two different jobs, which is why they cover different ground. `tickers` groups a traded symbol into one industry and aims to be COMPLETE: it badges and filters the stock pages, so a gap there reads to a visitor as a broken dataset. `committees` records which industries a committee has direct legislative jurisdiction over and is deliberately PARTIAL (see _committee_note). Together they let a member page say 'this member sits on Armed Services and disclosed trades in defense stocks'. That is a coincidence of two public facts. It is NOT a claim of conflict, insider knowledge, or causation, and no surface may present it as one. Hand-maintained: add a ticker when it is missing. An unlisted ticker is simply unclassified, which is why every surface publishes its coverage count.",

  "_committee_note": "Only committees with direct jurisdiction over an INDUSTRY are mapped. Tax and entitlement committees (Ways & Means, Senate Finance), the money committees (Appropriations, Budget), Judiciary (antitrust reaches every sector), Foreign Affairs and the government-wide oversight committees are deliberately absent: a flag that fires on almost every trade carries no information. An empty or missing entry means we map no sector to that seat, not that the seat is unimportant. Six industries below (consumer, industrial, autos, materials, realestate, business) map to NO committee on purpose. They exist so every stock page carries a badge; adding them to a committee would widen the member-page flag, which is a separate decision from classifying a stock.",

  "_label_note": "Keep every label at or under 21 characters. It has to fit the industry <select> on /tickers at a 320px viewport, where the longest option sets the control's width and a longer one pushes it off the page.",

